  "final_text": "I wouldn't move Q3 marketing to CRAFT yet. Fix the line first. Both of the VP's arguments depend on a misleading comparison: September's data has five weeks and August's has four. I recomputed everything from the CSVs, and the report's numbers match the data exactly. What they mean is different from what the VP says.\n\n**1. \"Units up 22.4%\" is the extra week, not demand.**\n| CRAFT | Aug (4 wks) | Sep (5 wks) | Change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Units/week | 1,872 | 1,832 | **−2.1%** |\n| Revenue/week | €825k | €825k | flat |\n\nWeekly CRAFT units stayed in a narrow 1,624–2,056 range across all nine weeks. Demand is flat, so there's no sign of momentum to build on.\n\n**2. \"Quality is already turning\" comes from the extra week and from fewer but larger NCRs.**\n- NCRs per week dropped from 6.0 to 4.6. But the 2.51-per-1,000 figure is lower mainly because September's unit count includes a fifth week.\n- Units affected per 1,000 units didn't change: **310 in August, 309 in September.** About **31% of CRAFT volume** is covered by an NCR in both months. For comparison: NEST is about 11%, PULSE about 9% and AURA about 6%.\n- The average NCR got bigger (97 → 123 units affected). More of them ended in scrap rather than rework (13 of 23, up from 11 of 24). Weld-crack NCRs went from 2 to 5.\n- \"No other line improved\" isn't true either. NEST's units affected per 1,000 fell from 118 to 105.\n\n**3. More demand means more production on a line where nearly a third of units hit an NCR.** That raises scrap and rework costs. If defects reach customers, it also risks the premium brand. CRAFT's €450 average price only matters if it carries a margin, and the data has no costs, so we can't say whether it does.\n\n**Recommendation:** Run a rework or root-cause push on CRAFT first, aimed at weld cracks, documentation and dimensional NCRs. Agree on an exit criterion, for example units affected per 1,000 falling well below 310 for 4–6 weeks in a row. Once CRAFT meets it, revisit marketing. If the VP wants to show something sooner, a small test on the higher-margin model would be a reasonable compromise, but only once there's cost data to identify it.\n\n**Limits of this data:**\n- NCRs measure production quality, not customer returns.\n- I used sales units as the denominator, which only approximates production volume.\n- Marketing budgets can't be attributed to a single line, so there's no evidence on what extra spend would actually achieve.\n- The data covers only two months.\n\nThe script I used is at `analyze.py`.",
